Let us exemplify how this works starting with \eqref{Stokes-comp-2nd} and computing the $S_{-k}^F$. For $n=1$, a power-series expansion of the left-hand side, together with the recursive action of the differential operator on the right-hand side, leads to  egin{eqnarray} \sum_{\ell=0}^{+\infty} \left(-S_{-1}^{Z}ight)^{\ell} 	au_{1}^{\ell+1} & = & 	au_{1} + \sum_{k_{1}=1}^{+\infty} S_{-k_{1}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{k_{1}+1} +  rac{1}{2!} \sum_{k_{1},k_{2}=1}^{+\infty} \left( k_{1}+1 ight) S_{-k_{1}}^{F} S_{-k_{2}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{k_{1}+k_{2}+1} + \ &+&  rac{1}{3!} \sum_{k_{1},k_{2},k_{3}=1}^{+\infty} \left( k_{1}+1 ight) \left( k_{1}+k_{2}+1 ight) S_{-k_{1}}^{F} S_{-k_{2}}^{F} S_{-k_{3}}^{F}\, 	au_{1}^{\sum_{i=1}^{3}k_{i}+1} + \cdots. 
onumber \end{eqnarray} 
oindent Let us compare equal powers of $	au_1^{\ell+1}$. For $\ell=0$ we get an identity; for the next few $\ell$'s we find  egin{eqnarray} \ell = 1: & -S_{-1}^{Z} & = S_{-1}^{F}, \ \ell = 2: & \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{2} & = S_{-2}^{F} +  rac{1}{2!} \left( 1+1 ight) \left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{2}, \ \ell = 3: & \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{3} & = S_{-3}^{F} +  rac{1}{2!} \left( 2+3 ight) S_{-1}^{F} S_{-2}^{F} +  rac{1}{3!} \left( 2 	imes 3 ight) \left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{3}. \end{eqnarray} 
oindent The first equation above, \eqref{Stokes-comp-one}, immediately yields $S_{-1}^{F} = -S_{-1}^{Z}$. Recursively solving the following equations one sees that \eqref{Stokes-comp-two} then yields $S_{-2}^{F} =0$ and \eqref{Stokes-comp-three} $S_{-3}^{F} =0$. This is a pattern which generalizes to higher powers of $	au_1$. Indeed, the first time the Stokes constant $S_{-k}^F$ will enter the game is when we write down the equation associated to $\ell=k$. But, at this stage, such equation will also include a term just in $\left(S_{-1}^Fight)^k$, while all other terms will be products of Stokes constants which always include at least one ootnote{The $m$-term contribution to the sum in \eqref{Stokes-comp-2nd} is given by  egin{equation}  rac{1}{m!} \prod_{i=1}^{m} \left(\, \sum_{k_{i}=1}^{+\infty} \left(\, \sum_{j=1}^{i-1}k_{j}+1\, ight) S_{-k_{i}}^{F}\, ight) 	au_{1}^{\sum_{j=1}^{m}k_{j}+1}. \end{equation} } $S_{-k'}^F$ with $1<k'<k$. For a general $\ell$ this looks like  egin{equation} \left( -S_{-1}^{Z} ight)^{\ell} = S_{-\ell}^{F} + \cdots +  rac{1}{\ell!}\, \ell!\left( S_{-1}^{F} ight)^{\ell}, \end{equation} 
oindent where the dots indicate terms involving Stokes constants $S_{-k}^F$, $1<k<\ell$, which were already set to zero from previous conditions. By induction, it follows that $S_{-k}^F=0$, for all $k>1$.

