For integer values of $\gamma$, the straightforward relation (with $n,m \in \BN^+$)  e  rac{s^n}{\Gamma \left( n+1 ight)} =  rac{md^m}{md s^m}\,  rac{s^{n+m}}{\Gamma \left( n+m+1 ight)} \ee 
oindent directly implies the relation between Borel transforms ootnote{As an historical aside, note that this sort of relations was probably first noticed in \cite{bpv78b}, but, unfortunately, the role of the standard representative---with its (simple) logarithmic ramified structure, and where resurgent analysis becomes most straightforward---was not emphasized enough to spark subsequent further analysis.}  e \CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma']} ] (s) = D_s^{\gamma-\gamma'} \CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s). \ee 
oindent Assuming that $\gamma-\gamma'$ is an integer and that $\gamma>\gamma'$, the operator $D_s^n$ is herein defined as the $n$th derivative with respect to $s$. The relation \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} also holds if $\gamma<\gamma'$, in which case one simply defines $D_s^n$ as the $n$th 	extit{primitive} ootnote{Each primitive yields an integration constant, and upon multiple primitivation one obtains a polynomial in $s$ with maximum degree $\gamma'-\gamma$. This polynomial is directly related to the first $n_0(\gamma)$ coefficients which were removed from the original asymptotic series $\Phi_{[\gamma]} (\lambda)$ in order to obtain the ``Borel transformable'' asymptotic series $\widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} (\lambda)$.} with respect to $s$. If $\gamma=\gamma'$ then $D_s^0$ is simply the identity. The assumption that $\gamma-\gamma'$ is an integer may be lifted, allowing \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} to be valid for a 	extit{continuous} parameter $\gamma \in \BR$. Let us show how this is done when ootnote{The case where $\gamma<\gamma'$ follows in complete analogy by simply inverting \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} and having the operator $D_s^{\gamma'-\gamma}$, which is now a 	extit{derivative}, acting on $\CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma']} ] (s)$.} $\gamma>\gamma'$. In this case we have to deal with a 	extit{fractional derivative}, where $D_s^\alpha$ is defined, when $0<\alpha<1$, as (see, 	extit{e.g.}, \cite{lot76})  egin{equation} D_s^\alpha f(s) =  rac{1}{\Gamma \left(1-\alphaight)}\,  rac{md}{md s} \int_0^s md t\,  rac{f(t)}{\left( s-t ight)^{\alpha}}. \end{equation} 
oindent This definition naturally extends to any real order $\gamma$ by setting $\gamma = \alpha + m$, with $\gamma \in \BR$, $m \in \BN_+$, and $\alpha$ as above, in which case  e D_s^\gamma f(s) = D_s^\alpha D_s^m f(s) = D_s^\alpha\,  rac{md^m f}{md s^m} (s). \ee 
oindent Applying the fractional derivative to each term in the definition of a Borel transform is straightforward; one only needs to know that, for $f(s) = s^ho$, $ho>-1$, one has  egin{equation} D_s^\alpha s^ho =  rac{\Gamma \left(ho+1ight)}{\Gamma \left(ho+1-\alphaight)}\, s^{ho-\alpha}. \end{equation} 
oindent The action of the fractional derivative $D_s^{\gamma-\gamma'}$ on the Borel transform $\CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s)$ in \eqref{eq:relation-between-borels-der} can then be easily computed making use of the above expression order-by-order in the series definition of $\CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s)$. One obtains  e D_s^{\gamma-\gamma'} \CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma]} ] (s) = \sum_{k=n_0(\gamma)}^{+\infty} \Phi_k\,  rac{s^{\alpha k +  eta + \gamma' - 1}}{\Gamma \left( \alpha k +  eta + \gamma' ight)} \approx \CB [ \widehat{\Phi}_{[\gamma']} ] (s). \ee
